WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:10.160
NASEER MOHAMMAD: Welcome.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.040
Welcome to Cloud OnAir, live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.090
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.725
My name is Naseer Mohammad.

00:00:17.725 --> 00:00:18.227
And--

00:00:18.227 --> 00:00:20.560
CHRIS PAGE: I'm Chris Page,
customer solutions architect

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.480
with Apigee, part
of Google Cloud.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.938
NASEER MOHAMMAD:
And today, we will

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:26.770
be talking about securing
your APIs with Apigee.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.600
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.680
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.660
So with that, let's get started.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:36.895
These are our names.

00:00:36.895 --> 00:00:39.300
These are our pictures.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.110
And here's the agenda.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.890
So today, we will go over
a brief overview of Apigee

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.380
and then do a deep dive
of Apigee Edge security.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.160
We'll follow that up
with a demo, a cool demo

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.440
that Chris is going to show.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.470
And then we will open it up
for questions and answers.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:58.970
So what is Apigee?

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.140
Apigee a comprehensive
API platform.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.180
It provides three core modules--

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.690
API management module that
is responsible for providing

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.440
development and
management of API proxies.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.960
Developer services
is a module that

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.830
is responsible for providing
a fully managed and highly

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.200
engaging developer portal.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.060
And then the analytics module--

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.380
the analytics module
will give you visibility

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.430
in the usage of
your APIs, thereby

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.700
providing you the visibility
for the effectiveness,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.040
for you to analyze the
effectiveness of your API

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.420
program.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.270
Apigee's also the
industry leader

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.480
for three consecutive years,
being on the top right

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.670
of the Magic Quadrant.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.780
And this is the
philosophy of Apigee--

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.760
thinking outside in.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.340
This philosophy at
Apigee challenges

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.310
traditional approaches
of API management.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.920
Traditionally, the thing
about API management

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.610
in terms of exposure,
you have services

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:02.690
which you want to expose.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.800
So you come up with APIs, and
you publish an API catalog.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.570
And you think the job is done.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.840
Well, not really.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.020
If you think about it from
an end user perspective

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.640
and work backwards from there,
that leads to better results.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.250
And also too, realizing
the importance

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:26.420
of each actor on this
chain and providing them

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.650
with enhanced experiences while
they interact with your API

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.390
platform becomes the key for
a successful API program.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:38.750
And using this platform and
using this design pattern,

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.840
we have shown success
to many of our clients.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.120
Now having said that, let's
change the role, right?

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.820
Let's change the
lens of this pattern.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.240
Let's look at this pattern
in terms of security.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.160
You know, you'll see we
cover a lot of ground

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.780
here, which opens up a room
for a lot of security controls

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.505
to be in place.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.240
So each actor on this
chain presents a unique set

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.980
of security
requirements, and we will

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.480
go through these requirements.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.740
And I want you guys, while I'm
walking through this flow here,

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.310
I want you to relate to your use
cases, your API use cases which

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.010
you are working on,
and see how you're

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.660
addressing the
security concerns which

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.770
I'm going to walk through here.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.750
So let's start this
exercise from left to right.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.940
On the left, while we publish
our APIs on the internet,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.510
how do we protect these
APIs from hackers?

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.550
When I say hackers, in
the back of your mind

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.710
you should be thinking
about SQL injection

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.250
threatening content
protection, denial of service,

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.640
et cetera, right?

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.220
So how can we protect
our APIs from hackers

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.310
who are trying to
bring your system down?

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:52.510
And how can we protect from data
scrapers, the data scrapers who

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.340
are trying to steal our data
for their competitive advantage?

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.130
Leading to the next
security concern,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.320
which is how do
you authenticate,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.020
and how do you know if
the incoming users are

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:09.220
legitimate users, and
if he or she really

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:13.960
have the needed permissions
to access these APIs?

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.779
Likewise, applications--
and enterprise

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.950
can have many applications.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.829
How can you identify
these applications?

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.760
How can you make
sure the APIs that

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.220
are invoked from
these applications

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.120
are legit APIs or all
APIs that are allowed

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.210
for this application to invoke?

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.140
And more importantly,
as we are talking,

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.210
or we are looking from a
security and [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.590
perspective, how do you ensure
that a decommissioned app can

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.770
no longer access these APIs?

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.740
Moving further towards the
right comes in developer.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.010
The app developers
create great apps.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.150
Now how can we help them
on-board on the API platform?

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.410
How can we register these app
developers on the API platform,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.280
providing them the
appropriate set of controls,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.220
or appropriate set of
APIs which they can

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:09.040
access, without much friction?

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.200
And that becomes a key.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.180
Moving for the
right in the chain

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.670
comes the APIs
and the API teams.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.490
Now this poses a
very interesting

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.740
and a critical
security concern, which

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.270
is how do you ensure that
the API developers know

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.790
what they are developing
with respect to security?

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.910
Are they opening up
any vulnerabilities

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.260
while they're
developing these APIs?

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.510
Are they adhering to
enterprise standards

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.670
while they're
developing these APIs?

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.530
That becomes a critical concern.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.300
And then finally
on the back end,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.430
how do you authenticate
to back end?

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.920
How do you pass
in to the back end

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.970
the information of the
original requester that

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.990
invoked this API?

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:56.660
And how do we address data
security on wire, in transit,

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:57.760
and at rest?

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.670
So these are the concerns that
comes up, and the list is long.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.270
Now let's see how Apigee
provides or addresses

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.970
some of the security concerns.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.650
Let's start from
left to right again.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:17.460
Apigee provides a behavior-based
threat protection model

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.490
to protect our APIs from
hackers and data scrapers.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.860
Apigee also protects our
APIs from threatening content

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.650
protection by providing
threatening content protection

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.820
policies--

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.650
attacks like SQL
injection, attacks

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.710
like cross-site scripting
attacks, XML bombs,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.010
or XML reference attacks.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.660
Apigee can have a
signature-based threat

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.720
protection policy deployed to
take care of these attacks.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.330
Apart from this, as you
publish your APIs on internet,

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:49.200
your APIs are prone
to spikes in traffic.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.650
Apigee effectively protects
against spikes of traffic,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:57.890
thereby preventing our back
end systems going down.

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:02.850
Apigee identifies
applications using API keys

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.510
and authenticate end
users using OAuth,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:10.410
the industry standard
authentication protocol.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.140
Apigee provides all five
grant types of OAuth.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:18.330
Apigee also can act as a fully
compliant authorization server

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.160
and an OAuth resource server.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.170
After addressing
the identification

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.770
and authentication
problems, the next challenge

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.050
would be the
authorization challenge,

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.630
which becomes a very
complex challenge

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.770
to address or to solve.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.970
Apigee addresses
this in two fashions.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:41.400
Apigee divides the
authorization space into two--

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.230
course-grained
authorizations and

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.390
fine-grained authorizations.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.460
For course-grained
authorization,

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.390
Apigee introduces this
concept called API products.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.930
API products help
you slice and dice

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.770
your APIs that makes
business sense.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.980
Now this API products,
in turn, will also

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:07.120
act as your
authorization enforcers.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.020
Similarly, you can have quota
enforcement on the products,

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:11.590
right?

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:15.320
Now when it comes to
fine-grained authorizations,

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:19.390
Apigee provides your
tools and policies

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.790
to implement scope-based
access control

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:26.110
policies and claims-based
access control policies,

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.940
leveraging OAuth
scopes and JWT tokens.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:34.419
Apigee provides logging
capabilities for auditability,

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:39.400
thereby covering identification,
authentication, authorization,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.039
and auditability
aspects of security.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.140
Apigee provides for
developers developer portal

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.300
and also management
APIs for them

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:53.731
to have a single sign on and
self-registration features

00:08:53.731 --> 00:08:55.480
for them to log-in to
the developer portal

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.360
and start using the APIs.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.120
Apigee can also work with
your identity provider

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.700
which you have at
your enterprise--

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:07.460
products like PingFederate,
SiteMinder, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.400
or what have you.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.530
As long as these products
support OAuth, SAML,

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.510
OpenID-based connect
protocols, Apigee

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.170
can integrate with your
identity providers,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:24.385
thereby providing single sign
on authentication for your app

00:09:24.385 --> 00:09:25.510
developers.

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.670
Apigee can also work
with your partners.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.900
Apigee can authenticate
your partners and also

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:36.280
the users and app developers or
internet using OpenID Connect

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:37.680
and SAML protocols.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.450
When it comes to
security and governance

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:46.380
and ensuring that your API
development team is really

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.440
developing APIs and not
opening up vulnerabilities,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.220
Apigee provides a cool feature,
which is known as flow hooks.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:58.650
Flow hooks externalizes
the security responsibility

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.960
from an API proxy developer.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.030
Thereby, a security expert can
go in and develop his policies

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.810
and auto-apply the
security policies

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.370
to the policies that are
developed your API proxy

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:12.930
developers.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.640
Thereby, API proxy
developers need not

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.220
worry about
implementing security.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.340
And it gets auto-enforced
while the API proxies

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.870
are being executed at runtime.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:28.050
Apigee provides
confidentiality for data online

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.610
and also data at rest,
providing the TLS capabilities

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:36.630
for one-way and two-way TLS for
both northbound traffic that

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.810
is coming into Apigee and
southbound traffic that

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:43.860
is leaving from Apigee
to your data centers.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.620
So these are some
of the features

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.540
which Apigee provides
with respect to security

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:48.450
at a high level.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.130
Now today, we will go over
signature-based threat

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.300
prediction module.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:57.260
That's where Chris will
start his demo and showcase

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:02.840
how you can leverage Apigee for
implementing threat protection.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.890
CHRIS PAGE: Thanks, Naseer.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.770
Let's talk a little bit more
details about that problem.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:12.030
API ecosystems can receive
malicious client attacks

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.540
from both internally
as well as externally.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.070
Those malicious clients
will send malformed requests

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.110
to your back end systems.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:22.140
The API proxy layer can
detect, block, and report

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.050
those malformed client
payloads and will

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:29.640
inspect the query parameters,
the headers, as well

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.970
as the payloads, and block
them or report them before they

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.820
hit the back end systems.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.810
As you can see from
this diagram below,

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.960
the malicious client is sending
both an HTTP post as well as

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:45.100
a GET in trying to attack
the back end service.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:48.460
Now Open Web Application
Security Project

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.310
is an open community
that focuses

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.900
on the best practices of
application and API governance

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.300
around security protocols.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.430
Every year, they
publish a top 10 list

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.387
of what enterprises
should be looking

00:12:02.387 --> 00:12:04.720
at when they're applying these
different types of threat

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.310
protections.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:09.650
We're going to focus today
on the number one, injection

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:10.150
flaws.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.150
It's been number one for
probably the last eight

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:13.900
years running.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.780
Input validation
is when the client

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:21.280
submits semantical or
syntactical different types

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:25.480
of data where the structure
can be nested structure,

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.260
like if you have a key value
pair in a JSON payload, what

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.340
happens if you nest multiple
arrays or multiple hashes

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:34.810
within that key value?

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.180
As well as
understanding the value

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:40.240
or the limit of those different
query parameter fields--

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:43.610
let's say I only request or
only require a limit name

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.970
in brewery in my API.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.440
And then the second
injection flaw

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.050
that we're going to take a
look at is SQL code injection.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:53.920
This is commonly noted where a
malicious client will actually

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:57.940
send malformed or
contextual-aware text

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:02.140
that can actually pinpoint and
hit your database layer where,

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:05.830
i.e., for a relational delete
from a particular schema,

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.510
like delete from resource.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.630
This can be applied for SQL code
injection, not just relational,

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:14.270
but also non-relational as well.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:17.890
So with the API itself in the
demo, we have a beer service.

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:22.630
And that beer service provides
a list of JSON details

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.100
about beers.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:26.830
Apigee will be the
enforcement point

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:31.181
so that you can have the
good requests go through.

00:13:31.181 --> 00:13:32.680
But the bad requests,
we're actually

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:36.280
going to block them and
return bad requests or 400

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.880
back to the client, as
well as log on them.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.380
So the goals of
this demo is create

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:46.370
an API proxy that protects
against OAuth injection flaws

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:47.810
from the client
request payloads,

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:49.727
as well as query parameters.

00:13:49.727 --> 00:13:51.560
We're going to keep it
as simple as possible

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.335
so that we can walk through and
show you all the details of all

00:13:54.335 --> 00:13:55.860
the policy itself.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.850
And third, let's report on the
usage so that we can identify,

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.640
and some of our API
teams or business folks

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.824
can actually learn about
from an analytic perspective.

00:14:04.824 --> 00:14:06.240
Now, let's take a
look at the API.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.370
So I'm logging-in to Apigee.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:18.060
And I am going to go into
Apigee Edge in the Management

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.260
interface.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.190
I'm going to take a look at
the API I built for this demo.

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.380
I call it the Beers On-Air Demo.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.530
And I'm going to go
over to the Develop tab.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.590
I'm going to pop this
open a little bit.

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.170
From the Develop
tab, you are going

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.930
to see a palette of
different configuration,

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:34.730
or what we call policies.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.260
Within each one
of these policies,

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:39.380
you can apply different
threat protections

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:40.910
or different enforcement.

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:42.740
The policies themselves
are broken up

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:47.120
into Traffic Management,
Security, as well as Mediation.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.740
And you have a whole
palette of easily

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:50.660
over 30 different policies
that you can apply

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.840
to your particular API proxy.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.480
For this particular proxy,
we're going to look initially

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.340
at JSON threat protection.

00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:01.540
And this has to do with
those input injections

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:05.240
or validations of the client
sending malformed request

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:06.364
payloads.

00:15:06.364 --> 00:15:07.780
So in this particular
example, I'm

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:10.100
going to look for a
particular container depth.

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.520
I'm going to make sure there's
no arrays within my structure.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.430
I'm going to make sure there's
only three keys in my object

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:17.250
