00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.360
I'm going to make sure the
object length of those names

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.067
is only 50 characters.

00:15:22.067 --> 00:15:23.900
I'm also going to make
sure the value is not

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:26.460
over 255 characters.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:29.760
Now, the API itself--

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:30.950
let me just open this up.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.590
And of course, the API
doesn't want to work.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:43.350
There we go.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:46.050
My API is returning
a list of JSON data

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.000
about that information.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.760
Now, I already went ahead
and created a client.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.390
I have a simple single-page app.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:57.690
Once I go to my single-page app,
I need to authenticate my user.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:01.980
So I'm just going to do a
simple demo user for today.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.661
We're going to do--
let's do a JWT token.

00:16:04.661 --> 00:16:06.660
Once I have my token, I'm
going to actually make

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:10.330
a successful request
to that back end API.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.360
Now with the platform
itself, we also

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.060
have the ability to do
real-time debugging.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:17.730
So if I go open
to my proxy again,

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:20.880
we have a concept
called Trace, which

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:24.709
allows me to do real-time trace
debugging of that proxy itself.

00:16:24.709 --> 00:16:27.000
So let me start that trace
section so that you actually

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:30.780
can see the successful requests
going through initially.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.190
I'll hit that refresh again.

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:33.564
And actually, I'll
send a filter.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.189
[? Mean ?] equals L. You'll
see that it's filtered.

00:16:38.189 --> 00:16:39.730
And when I actually
go into my trace,

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.020
I actually can walk
through each one

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:43.660
of those individual policies
that I've defined for my threat

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.780
protection and make sure that
everything is going through,

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:49.642
and the client is
receiving those requests.

00:16:49.642 --> 00:16:51.600
So that's an example of
the successful request.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.725
Well, let's take a look
and let's try and implement

00:16:53.725 --> 00:16:56.680
that JSON threat protection.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:59.730
So I'll reset this.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:00.900
Let's create a new beer.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.780
Let's just call
it a sample beer.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:07.660
We'll have the Google
brewery, and we'll

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.760
make it On-Air for the style.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.960
Once I make that success,
you'll see my new beers there.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:15.579
I'll go back to my trace.

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:17.540
You'll see that the
201 was created,

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:21.250
so it was successful request
for actually a post coming

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.131
right there.

00:17:22.131 --> 00:17:23.589
Now actually, I'm
going to go back.

00:17:23.589 --> 00:17:25.575
I'm going to send a
malformed-- or I'm

00:17:25.575 --> 00:17:29.200
going to send a character string
over 255 characters, which

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:31.390
I defined in that JSON
threat protection.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:33.160
A lot of the times,
clients will have that

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:34.600
on client-facing validation.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:36.520
But if that's not
there, this is going

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:39.940
to simulate what happens when
a malicious client's going

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:41.050
to send that.

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:44.090
So let's do a random characters.

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:46.930
Let's do 256.

00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:49.532
Pipe that too.

00:17:49.532 --> 00:17:50.990
So I'm going to
change the name now

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:55.190
to that random array
of 256 characters.

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:58.310
And I'll just put a bunch
of brewery and style

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:00.050
for my parameters.

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:03.500
Immediately, once I
clicked Submit or Save,

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.740
I receive that 400
error on my client.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:07.490
You'll see there's
details within the body

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.910
of the response coming back that
I'm displaying for this demo.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:13.640
Basically, the JSON threat
protection was caught,

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:16.640
and there is exceeded
string length at line 1.

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:21.140
Once I go back to my
real-time trace debugger,

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.030
you'll see that
400 that came in.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:26.540
And I can walk through exactly
