00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:28.340
within my Policy window.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.220
You'll see that this
little icon right here,

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:34.250
"Execution error for JSON threat
protection," basically that

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:40.520
is where my API proxy was
able to inspect the payload

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:43.470
and realize that that string
character was over that limit.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.300
So that was the first example.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:48.110
Let's take a look at
the second example.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.260
So second example, we're going
to do parameter validation.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:52.910
And parameter
validation, I'm going

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:56.030
to use the ability within
our API manager platform

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.760
to call out JavaScript
and do some business

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:02.220
logic to validate those
particular parameters that

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:03.334
are coming in.

00:19:03.334 --> 00:19:05.750
You'll see that this policy
is actually going to call this

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:08.450
parameter_validation.js.

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:10.167
I have a set of
valid parameters,

00:19:10.167 --> 00:19:11.750
as well as some of
the pagination that

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:13.141
is in my application.

00:19:13.141 --> 00:19:15.390
And I'm going to check the
length of those parameters,

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.150
making sure the
length is not greater

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.530
than what is white listed
here, as well as I'm going

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:20.860
to iterate over the parameters.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:23.276
And if any of the parameters
that are sent from the client

00:19:23.276 --> 00:19:26.060
are going I'm fail out, and
fail gracefully, and send back

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.840
that error to the client.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:29.510
So let's try this one out.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:30.605
My trace is still running.

00:19:30.605 --> 00:19:32.540
I'm going to go
to my application.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:37.620
Let's go to-- you'll
see before that I

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:40.170
can filter with
a successful name

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:43.740
parameter as a white
listed URL parameter.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.720
What happens if I do
something like this?

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:50.580
Users-- oh, just like
that, another 400 error.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:54.330
This time, it's just like
before-- the JSON validation

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:54.900
error.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:56.816
This time, I'm getting
a different validation.

00:19:56.816 --> 00:19:58.290
I'm getting
parameter validation.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:02.640
And they'll see that this
user is not a valid parameter.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:08.180
Likewise, when I go back
to the trace debugger,

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:10.050
I can actually
trace through here.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:13.340
And when the JavaScript
was executed,

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:15.780
and actually it's going
to trigger a variable.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.930
And that variable is going to
set a custom error message.

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:21.580
This custom error message
is called raise parameter

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.540
validation fault. So
within your policies,

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:27.270
you can use out-of-the-box ones,
or you can define additional

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:30.270
custom ones, like I did
here and with JavaScript.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.900
That's example number two.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:36.830
Let's take a look at
the third example.

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:39.330
Let me go back to my flow.

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:42.610
Last example is called
SQL code protection.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:45.970
And SQL code is that
common regex or string

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:48.520
that malicious
clients try to inject

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:52.420
to manipulate the data
structures of your database

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:53.290
layer.

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.530
And a lot of
[? allocations ?] leverage

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.000
ORM or different
types of libraries

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.450
to escape out some
of those regexes

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.610
or escape out some of
those single quotes.

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:05.560
Sometimes, that's
not always the case.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:07.420
And sometimes, you
need to enforce that

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:10.090
within the API proxy layer.

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:12.280
So a SQL code protection--

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:15.940
I'm actually going to check for
different regexes, like DELETE,

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:21.220
EXECUTE, or DROP, INSERT, or
various different SQL regex

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:26.680
or SQL code key parameters
that a malicious client would

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:27.870
be sending.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:28.840
So let's double-check.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:31.315
Let's see if my trace
is still running.

00:21:31.315 --> 00:21:33.725
Yep, I've still got
some time going here.

00:21:33.725 --> 00:21:34.600
I'm going to go back.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.670
I'm going to reset
my application.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:39.760
You'll see that I have a
successful request coming in.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.730
This time, let's simulate a
malicious client that's going

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:48.100
to try to DELETE from Beers.

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:50.800
Let's say if it tries to
delete from my Beer schema.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:53.830
Oh, just like before,
I got another 400

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:56.980
back from a bad request
from my API proxy layer.

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:59.440
You'll see that the details
in that false string,

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.510
the regular expression
threat detected

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:03.550
was the SQL code protection.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:10.050
And just like before, if I walk
through my real-time debugger

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:13.725
within my trace section, you'll
see that this time, request

00:22:13.725 --> 00:22:16.710
was received for client,
validated the client,

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:20.190
went through, JSON threat
protection was passed,

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:21.940
parameter validation was passed.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:25.260
This time, SQL code protection
was actually triggered here,

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:28.140
and the proxy was hit.

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:35.630
Now that was how we
enforce different policies

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:38.720
from a threat
protection standpoint.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:42.680
Let's take a look at what's
available for reporting

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.690
and the third thing
of how we want

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:48.860
to see how we report on
those different metrics.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:52.070
So within Apigee,
you have the ability

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:55.730
to send custom reports
or custom logging

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:58.290
within your proxy layer.

00:22:58.290 --> 00:23:00.740
So with these individual
threat protections,

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:03.620
I'm actually going to look
for a particular string

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:08.330
or particular validation when
the applications send it.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:09.980
So this error code,
I'm actually going

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:12.800
to capture this error
code within my policy

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.660
and then send that to
Apigee's rich analytics engine

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:18.200
so that we can have
real-time dashboards

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:22.040
on the amount of threats that
are coming into my system.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.570
That is one way to
capture the analytics.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:28.400
The other way would be to send
to your additional southbound

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:31.280
or northbound systems of
your monitoring systems--

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:35.210
your sys logs, your
SNMP agents, or any

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:36.710
of the event-driven
agents that can

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.860
capture these different
information to report from a VI

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:41.330
perspective.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:43.460
So let's take a
look at my policies

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:45.680
that I have added for that.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:50.330
Within my fault rules,
I have a policy called,

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:53.330
Extract fault error codes.

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.130
And this policy
is actually going

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:58.790
to look at the JSON payload
or the JSON structure

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:01.160
when a fault is triggered.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:04.530
So I'm actually going
to use JSON path here.

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:06.380
The JSON path schema
is actually going

00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:09.200
to look for that error
code and actually capture

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:15.950
that as a separate variable,
called fault error code, here.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:18.110
Once I capture that
error code, I'm

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:21.290
actually going to use the
statistics collector policy

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:25.370
within Apigee that allows me to
send custom statistic metrics

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.189
to our analytics engine.

00:24:27.189 --> 00:24:28.730
So the fault collector
policy, you'll

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:33.380
see that I'm actually going to
create a new statistics value,

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:35.906
called fault_errorcode,
which is going

00:24:35.906 --> 00:24:37.280
to reference that
variable I just

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.755
created from that JSON path.

00:24:39.755 --> 00:24:42.980
Then I'm going to store that
into our analytics engine.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:46.610
So if I go back to
my flow within here,

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:48.440
you'll actually see
that those policies

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:53.360
are being executed on every
fault that's being triggered.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:57.020
So if I click Next
within my trace debugger,

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:58.910
you'll see that that
SQL code protection

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:00.830
was triggered right here.

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:08.020
And then if I scroll down,
you will see there is--

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:08.680
where is it?

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:15.620
I am going to-- let me
just walk over once more.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:16.590
Here it is.

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.950
So here is the error
content that was triggered

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:20.810
from that SQL code protection.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.750
You'll see that that JSON
structure is right here.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:26.090
Here is that fault
error code that I'm

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:28.550
capturing from that policy.

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:31.940
So this fault error code
will be unique for each one

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:35.450
of my policies so that I
can report on accordingly.

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:38.660
Once I hit Next, you'll see
the statistics collector is

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:42.110
actually going to
take that policy,

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:46.520
and then there's an additional--

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:48.860
if a particular
error message occurs,

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:50.890
I don't want it to
send a 500 back.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:55.880
But so let's go back
to our analysts engine.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:26:00.860
So within Apigee, you have the
ability to see client traffic.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:03.740
You have the ability to
see user-based traffic,

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:06.710
proxy-based traffic,
product-based traffic.

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:08.120
You have a rich
analytics engines

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:11.030
that allows you to dive in
into each individual policy

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:14.750
to learn more about how your
API program or your API proxies

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:16.110
are performing.

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:22.450
So if I go into that custom
reporting that I created,

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.950
I have already created a report,
called fault error codes.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.200
That fault error
codes, actually,

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.270
you'll see right
here, is reporting

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:34.800
on each individual fault that
I enabled within my policies.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:36.780
You'll see that
this invalid access

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:39.600
token was when I was not
authenticated or authorized

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:40.740
from my client app.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:46.200
But more specifically, here
is the JSON threat protection.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.240
Here was the
parameter validation,

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:49.980
and here's the regex protection.

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:51.570
So I can dive into
each one of these

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.850
