00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:08.190
The next question in here is, as
new OWASP rules are published,

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.890
can the API threat
protection policies

00:33:10.890 --> 00:33:12.870
be updated dynamically?

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.284
Wow.

00:33:14.284 --> 00:33:16.200
So this is a very
interesting question, right?

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.320
So the bigger question
behind the question

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:24.090
really is if, let's say
you develop an API proxy

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:31.260
and have the regular expression
which we got from OWASP

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:34.200
injected into the proxy itself.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:36.480
It's going to be
kind of hard coded

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:38.230
for that particular
regular expression.

00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:41.020
Now, as in when
OWASP is publishing

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:43.800
new regular expirations, how
can we make it more dynamic?

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:45.690
And this a very
interesting question.

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:51.010
One approach you can take
is, rather than hard coding

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:52.930
the regular expression
in the proxy itself,

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:55.030
you might want to refer
to that regular expression

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:56.410
with a key value, right?

00:33:56.410 --> 00:33:58.630
So you might want to store
your regular expression

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:02.410
in our key value map
storage, wherein you can

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:04.280
refer to a regular expression.

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:07.900
For example, let's say it was
SQL injection specifically

00:34:07.900 --> 00:34:09.790
for insert attack, right?

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:11.206
So the key would be [?

00:34:11.206 --> 00:34:13.630
SQLInjection_insertAttack.
?] And then you can refer

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:17.130
the value from the KVM.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:19.730
And that gets pulled
in dynamically.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:22.300
Now the other part
to this would be,

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:25.420
how would you hook up
your OWASP updating

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:27.880
your regular
expression to Apigee?

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:30.050
That, you can do
through management API.

00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:33.760
So Apigee provides management
API capabilities for you

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:35.409
to update key value maps.

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:37.310
Or anything which
you can do on a UI,

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:39.340
there's a management
API for that, right?

00:34:39.340 --> 00:34:42.520
So in this case, you would
leverage the management API

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:48.580
and tie in OWASP's updates
to trigger an update

00:34:48.580 --> 00:34:49.750
in your key value map.

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:53.300
Thereby, you need not really
re-deploy your policies

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.150
or recreate your proxies, right?

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:59.482
This becomes dynamic
and adds a lot of value.

00:34:59.482 --> 00:35:00.190
CHRIS PAGE: Yeah.

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:01.960
So from a programmatic
standpoint,

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:03.937
that can be automated
for your CIC pipeline.

00:35:03.937 --> 00:35:05.020
NASEER MOHAMMAD: Oh, yeah.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:05.260
CHRIS PAGE: Great.

