00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:13.270
Also too, some of
your clients might

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:16.490
want to come and authenticate
to you over OAuth.

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:19.690
And some might want to
authenticate to you over SAML.

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:23.080
And a single policy can't
cater to all of these needs,

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:26.980
and thereby, leading to
a multi-profile security.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:29.680
The way you will
achieve this in Apigee

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:34.910
is by what is known as
profile-based security.

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:38.200
In Apigee, you can define
multiple security profiles.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:40.180
And these security
profiles can be

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:42.970
configured for your customer.

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:44.470
I'm just giving
this example, right?

00:37:44.470 --> 00:37:47.650
Let's say Bank of America is
making a call to your APIs.

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:50.320
And then Wells Fargo is
making a call to your APIs.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:53.950
And Bank of America wants to
authenticate to you over SAML.

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:57.490
And Wells Fargo wants to
authenticate to you over OAuth.

00:37:57.490 --> 00:38:00.130
The cool thing that Apigee
does-- it first of all,

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:03.970
starts your security
process with identification.

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:07.570
You identify who the
caller is, and then you

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:08.800
authenticate the caller.

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:10.600
And then you authorize
the caller, right?

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:11.266
CHRIS PAGE: Yep.

00:38:11.266 --> 00:38:13.330
NASEER MOHAMMAD: So once
the identification part

00:38:13.330 --> 00:38:16.510
is done, at Apigee, you would
know who the end user is.

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:19.180
In this case, it would be
Bank of America, right?

00:38:19.180 --> 00:38:22.240
And then you would
direct your flow hook

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:26.140
to a security profile that is
applicable for Bank of America.

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:29.490
Thereby, might be Bank
of America wants SAML,

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:31.910
and you go through
SAML authentication

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:33.400
and then authorizations.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:35.080
Similarly, if it
is Wells Fargo, you

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:37.300
would let them
through other channel.

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:40.340
And that's actually a
very good pattern, right?

